raging this week's strike  will be arrested but these threats are  falling short with so many Iranians  willing to risk their lives and their  freedom to call for an end to the regime  because Abdullahi is an Iranian  journalist and photographer she took  part in the mass protests in her Tehran  and fled to Germany just a few weeks ago  thank you so much for joining us on DW  today I'd like to start by asking you to  share with us if you would some of your  experiences when you were out on the  streets protesting  hello and thank you  uh well um before right before I came  here  um I participated in protests in a  street as well as many people  and  I can talk about one experience that I  had one day in a protests in the streets  well we went to the streets and uh  we were walking  after minutes of walking  we looked behind us and there were so  many people behind us like 200 people  and we were all walking slowly in  silence  and then just in a second the guards  came and uh well we had we we ran away  we had to run away  um  well and uh I got shot by a paintball  bullets  and they hit me I fell down on the  ground and  oh  one guy he opened his car and shouted  that uh come to my car and so I went to  his car and uh I was in the car and the  guards came around the car and started  shooting to the car and hitting the car  and I was just one glass away from  resting getting getting arrested by the  police  gazelle your description there is really  powerful I can I can hear in your voice  just how how terrifying a situation that  was and so I'd like to ask you why you  joined the protests clearly you knew  people know the danger they're they're  putting themselves uh into what made you  so determined to protest on the streets  um well first of all uh my mother she's  a political prisoner and she has been a  women's rights activist in Iran and she  she has been in prison for two years  so uh  this is one reason and the other reason  that's most more important reason is  that  I'm one of so many people in Iran that  wanted to see a change  and  well everyone is now participating in  protests for the cause of change to see  the change  and  we only have our lives to exchange for  freedom  gazelle the the protests have been going  on for nearly three months now do you  feel like they're making a difference do  you and do you feel that they're as  strong as they were when they first  started or in fact are they even  stronger  um  uh of course from the start uh people we  started uh so strong and still going so  strong uh people are getting smarter day  by day  changing uh the vase they're protesting  they're striking and uh yeah  everyone is getting stronger in Iran  people  has the power now  can I ask you about the attention that  is being focused on these protests the  they're being watched across the world  um are activists in Iran aware of of the  solidarity and support and and really  what would you say what else needs to be  done from the outside to support the  protests in Iran  oh well um All the known activists in  Iran uh they got arrested since the  protests started uh even uh just the  people uh just the normal people uh that  were fighting for the for their rights  uh got arrested  so people in the streets who are  participating in the protests and  everything uh yeah  the solidarity here gives them hope they  see the videos and the protests here and  it just give them so so much hope  it it makes them stronger when they see  all these people uh  outside of country outside of Iran  Abdullahi thank you so much for talking  to us today and we wish you all the very  best  thank you
 

